>> title: Pad Krapow
>> category: Noodles & Stir-Fries
>> tags: pork, thai, chiles, eggs, wok
>> counters: Thai Kitchen
>> aka: pad kaprao, pad gaprao, pad kra pao, krapow, holy basil stir-fry, pad krapow moo, basil stir fry
>> servings: 2
>> time: 20 min
>> step.1: pound to a rough paste
>> step.2: stir the sauce
>> step.3: fry the paste 1 min, then the pork 3 min
>> step.4: toss off the heat
>> step.5: fry the eggs 2 min, and spoon it over

Pound @Thai chiles{6}(stemmed) and @garlic{5%cloves} to a rough paste in a #mortar{}. Six is a working lunch's worth; the number is the dial, and it is the only thing anyone changes.

Stir @oyster sauce{1%Tbs}, @thin soy sauce{2%tsp}, @dark soy sauce{1/2%tsp}, @fish sauce{2%tsp}, @sugar{1%tsp} and @water{2%Tbs} together in a bowl.

Fry @&(~2)chile paste{} in @vegetable oil{2%Tbs} in a #wok{} over high heat for ~fry{1%min}, then add @ground pork{8%oz}(225 g) and fry it hard for ~stirfry{3%min}, until some of it catches and browns.

Toss @&(~1)pork{} with @&(~2)sauce{} and @holy basil{2%cups}(leaves, packed) off the heat, so the basil wilts without stewing.

Fry @eggs{2%large} in @vegetable oil{1/2%cup}(120 mL; deep enough to float them) for ~fry{2%min}, until the whites blister and go lacy at the edges, then set them on @jasmine rice{3%cups}(cooked, hot) with @&(~1)pork{} beside them. The crisp-edged egg is not a garnish — khai dao is what makes this the plate the whole country eats at lunch.
